🔹 Before (Traditional switch statement)
String day = "MONDAY";
int num;

switch (day) {
    case "MONDAY":
        num = 1;
        break;
    case "TUESDAY":
        num = 2;
        break;
    default:
        num = 0;
}

❌ Problems with old style

Required break (easy to forget → bugs)

More boilerplate

Not an expression (can’t assign directly)

Less readable

🔹 After (Java 12+ / 14+ / 17+)

Your modern version (switch expression):

int num = switch (day) {
    case "MONDAY" -> 1;
    case "TUESDAY" -> 2;
    default -> 0;
};

🧠 Interview one-liner

“Switch expressions allow switch to return a value, eliminate fall-through, and improve readability using arrow syntax.”